
                         relibc on Genode

relibc (Redox's C library, in Rust) as the C library of Genode components,
instead of Genode's FreeBSD-based libc. A Genode repository: add it to
REPOSITORIES in the build directory's etc/build.conf.

relibc is not forked here: tools/get-relibc clones upstream relibc and
applies what this repository keeps of it, the Genode platform module
(relibc/src/platform/genode) and two patches (patches/). SETUP describes
everything from a clean machine on: host packages, Genode's tool chain,
Genode, Rust, the build directory and the tests.

  relibc/     what goes into relibc: its Genode platform module
              (relibc/src/platform/genode, the place it has in relibc)
  patches/    the rest of relibc's changes: generic fixes, and the few
              Genode lines outside the platform module; the base commit
  src/        the Genode side: the shim (src/lib/relibc_genode), tests,
              ports that need a different build file with relibc
  lib/        Genode build files: relibc as libc.lib.so, the shim library
  include/    headers for code written against Genode's FreeBSD libc
  run/        test scenarios
  recipes/    depot recipes (relibc_hello, for Sculpt)
  tools/      get-relibc, save-relibc, build-relibc, mk-symbols
  var/        build output of tools/build-relibc (not in git)

The details are under "Layout" at the end.


Status
------

Runs on NOVA (Sculpt's kernel), hw and base-linux, with relibc's C
headers:

* stdio, malloc, arguments and environment from the config, exit codes
* threads (pthread_create/join/cancel, mutexes, condition variables, timed
  waits, per-thread errno)
* files and directories of the component's VFS: open/read/write/lseek,
  pread/pwrite, O_APPEND, ftruncate, fsync, stat/fstat/lstat,
  opendir/readdir, mkdir, rmdir, unlink, rename, access, chdir/getcwd and
  relative paths, symbolic links (symlink, readlink, followed in paths,
  O_NOFOLLOW, ELOOP), as Genode's libc follows them; mmap of files
  (MAP_PRIVATE, a copy, as Genode's libc), fcntl locks and flock (granted:
  one component), fstatvfs
* Genode's libc config: '+ libc' with stdin, stdout, stderr (files of the
  VFS, else no input and the LOG), cwd, rtc and rng (VFS files)
* processes, as with Genode's libc: fork (a child component of the same
  binary, which copies the parent's state), execve (the program replaced
  within the component, scripts with "#!"), posix_spawn (a child component
  of another program, with file actions), waitpid/wait4 (WNOHANG),
  SIGCHLD, getpid/getppid, FD_CLOEXEC (fcntl, O_CLOEXEC, SOCK_CLOEXEC)
* pipes: of the VFS's pipe plugin with '+ libc | pipe: /pipe' (as Genode's
  libc), which reach forked and spawned children and other components,
  else between the component's threads; O_NONBLOCK; socketpair (AF_UNIX
  streams within the component), dup/dup2/F_DUPFD
* fds from the config, as with Genode's libc: '+ libc | + fd | id: 3 |
  path: /file | readable: yes | seek: 5'
* poll, select and epoll (pipes, files, standard I/O)
* sockets, TCP and UDP over IPv4, through the VFS's socket file system as
  with Genode's libc ('+ libc | socket: /socket', the IP stack lwip or
  lxip in the VFS, of the component or of a VFS server): socket, bind,
  listen, accept, connect (also non-blocking), send/recv(from/to/msg), MSG_PEEK,
  MSG_DONTWAIT, getsockname/getpeername, the socket options the IP stack
  keeps, poll/select/epoll on sockets; the nameserver for relibc's
  resolver from the socket file system ('<socket>/nameserver', or
  '+ libc | nameserver_file'), as with Genode's libc (not tested against
  a DNS server yet)
* signals within the component, as with Genode's libc: sigaction (also
  SA_SIGINFO, SA_RESETHAND, SA_NODEFER), raise, kill of the component
  itself, masks per thread, sigpending, sigsuspend and pause, sigtimedwait,
  alarm and setitimer (ITIMER_REAL), POSIX timers (timer_create with
  SIGEV_SIGNAL); delivered when the program enters relibc's platform
  (raise, kill, sigprocmask, sigsuspend, sleeping, poll/select/epoll,
  which a signal interrupts with EINTR), default actions end the component
* terminals of the VFS (its terminal plugin): isatty, tcgetattr/tcsetattr
  and TIOCGWINSZ, from the terminal's info node as Genode's libc reads it
* clocks and sleeping (Timer); CLOCK_REALTIME from the '+ libc | rtc'
  file (Genode's rtc VFS plugin) or, with '+ relibc | rtc: yes', an Rtc
  session; getrandom from the '+ libc | rng' file, else /dev/random of the
  VFS if it has one (e.g. vfs_jitterentropy)

run/relibc_posix.run checks most of that (86 checks), run/relibc_process.run
the processes (in a build directory with relibc as the libc, below),
run/relibc_hello.run the basics. It runs in Sculpt, too (see "In Sculpt").

  tools/get-relibc && tools/build-relibc         # once, and after relibc changes
  cd ../genode/build/x86_64
  make run/relibc_posix                          # NOVA in QEMU (the build dir's default)
  make run/relibc_posix KERNEL=hw    BOARD=pc
  make run/relibc_posix KERNEL=linux BOARD=linux

Not yet: signals between components (kill of a child, SIGCHLD aside) and
SIGEV_THREAD timers (Linux's sigevent, which relibc uses, has no place for
the function), fork from another thread than main's, posix_spawn's
signal attributes (a spawned child starts with the default mask and
actions), process groups and sessions, stopped children, the VFS's OSS
and tap ioctls (Genode's libc translates FreeBSD's SNDCTL_* and
TAPGIFNAME), IPv6 and named Unix-domain sockets (as with Genode's libc),
shared mappings of files and across fork.
Everything not implemented returns ENOSYS.

Limits: C code must not declare __thread variables itself (that
would be fs-relative); errno and relibc's own thread-locals are fine.
Pipes and sockets within the component (not of the VFS) do not reach
children; a child reopens the parent's files by their paths, with their
positions, but has positions of its own from then on (as with Genode's
libc).
pthread_cancel stops the thread at once, as Genode's libc does (relibc
sends a signal on Linux, which Genode does not deliver): asynchronous, so
a lock the thread holds stays locked, and its cleanup handlers do not run.


Compared with Genode's libc
---------------------------

Measured on base-linux, both libcs in their own build directory:

* Stock test packages that use libc (31; run/pkg_test.run): Genode's libc
  passes 26 (the other 5 need more than the runner offers, or fail with
  either libc). relibc passes 20 of those 26, now with fork, execve and
  pipes. Of the 6 others, libc and kqueue test FreeBSD's API (they do not
  compile), libc_connect_* (4) use FreeBSD's sockaddr layout.
* Exported C functions (libc and libm): 1191 for Genode's libc, 1193 for
  relibc, 954 in common. Only Genode's: mostly FreeBSD's own (sysctl,
  kqueue, fts, vis, getfsent, cget*), and of POSIX: fork's family (vfork,
  wait3), getcontext/makecontext/swapcontext, ftw/nftw, hsearch/lsearch,
  open_memstream, wordexp, SysV semaphores, sem_open, aio, fenv,
  clock_nanosleep, posix_fadvise, readdir_r. Only relibc's: the *at
  functions, epoll, POSIX timers, sigtimedwait/sigwait, C11 threads,
  pthread barriers and spin locks, shm_open, *_l locale variants.
* Memory: relibc's data was 1 MiB larger, nearly all chrono-tz's time-zone
  table, which Genode copies into every component (relocated data). With
  only UTC and Etc/* built in, a component's RAM at start went from
  1788 KiB to 780 KiB (measured in libc_udp_unreachable_lxip); threads
  have Genode's libc's default stack size. libc.lib.so is 4.2 MiB (with
  openlibm) against 2.3 MiB (libc and libm).
* Code to maintain for Genode: the shim (5300 lines of C++) and relibc's
  platform module (1400 lines of Rust), against Genode's libc glue (18700
  lines) on FreeBSD's libc.

Where Genode's libc is ahead: OSS and tap ioctls, long double
(relibc's printf supports only %La of it, strtold goes through double), locales (relibc has C and
locale files). Where relibc is
ahead: a Linux ABI and glibc-like headers (ports configured for Linux),
POSIX additions Genode's libc lacks (above), and an upstream that is not
Genode's to maintain.


relibc as the libc
------------------

relibc can take the place of Genode's FreeBSD-based libc for everything
built in a build directory that lists this repository first:

  REPOSITORIES := $(GENODE_DIR)/../relibc-genode $(REPOSITORIES)

(genode/build/x86_64_relibc does; the build system takes the first
repository with a file, so this one's lib/mk/{libc,posix,libm}.mk,
lib/import/import-{libc,posix}.mk and lib/symbols/* win.) Then libc.lib.so
is relibc with openlibm and the shim, posix.lib.so is Genode's own
posix/construct.cc, and Genode's libc API (Libc::Component, Libc::Env,
Libc::with_libc, libc/args.h) works as before. Programs need no changes.

After a change of relibc or the shim, in that build directory:

  ../../../relibc-genode/tools/build-relibc
  make lib/libc lib/posix lib/libm
  ../../../relibc-genode/tools/mk-symbols $PWD      # the ABI lists, if exports changed

Compile status (all targets of the Genode tree that use libc or posix):
of the 85 outside Qt (whose ports are not prepared here), 77 compile, one
is for 32-bit only (gmp's test), and the 7 others test FreeBSD's API (see
"Known gaps"), among them every application, server and driver: lighttpd,
the wifi driver with wpa_supplicant, stdcxx, e2fsprogs, mupdf, ...

Stock Genode test packages that pass unchanged (run/pkg_test.run below):
stdcxx, pthread and libc_vfs_ram (base-linux and NOVA), libc_vfs_fs,
libc_getenv, libc_deferred_unlink, ldso, libc_with_libc,
libc_many_writes, and over the network tcp_bulk_lwip and tcp_bulk_lxip
(8 MiB both ways, the IP stack in the component and in a VFS server),
libc_udp_unreachable_lwip and _lxip, libc_execve, libc_fork and
libc_pipe (the latter three also on NOVA, with the VFS's pipes across
components). (The tcp_bulk tests hang now and
then on base-linux, with "Packet dropped" at the NIC bridge: with Genode's
libc as often, 2 of 8 runs. With relibc, core warns "invalid
signal-context capability" when a component exits: not yet understood.)
libc_alarm passes (base-linux and NOVA); libc_connect_* fail as their server
(test/netty) initializes sockaddr_in for FreeBSD's layout (sin_len first).

Applications: run/relibc_lighttpd_fetchurl.run (Genode's
ports/run/lighttpd_fetchurl.run with every component built here, not
taken from the depot) serves 1 MiB with lighttpd and downloads it with
fetchurl (curl), both on relibc, over lwip and the NIC router. ieee754 runs; its output differs
from Genode's FreeBSD reference where relibc prints NaN as "-nan" (as
glibc) and long double through double, and where relibc rounds correctly
and the reference does not (pow(0.5, 0.5) = 0.707107).

run/pkg_test.run runs one of Genode's test packages as depot_autopilot
would, from its recipe (program, libraries, config, success pattern), for
tests that need no other components:

  make run/pkg_test TEST=test-pthread KERNEL=linux BOARD=linux

Header compatibility: include/relibc-compat comes first. It wraps relibc
headers where Genode's code relies on FreeBSD behaviour (fd_set through
sys/types.h and unistd.h, struct tm through sys/stat.h, no PAGE_SIZE in
limits.h, _*_T_DECLARED marks, float.h from the compiler) and provides
FreeBSD and glibc headers that ports configured for Genode's libc expect
(sys/limits.h, sys/disk.h, sys/disklabel.h, sys/mount.h, mntent.h,
net/if_dl.h, net/ethernet.h, xlocale.h, byteswap.h, ...), each
honest about Genode (no mount table, the disk size from the VFS). relibc's
headers select their Linux ABI by __relibc_linux_abi__ instead of
__linux__ (tools/build-relibc rewrites cbindgen's mapping), so that ports
configured for Genode's libc do not take Linux-only code paths. The C
functions relibc lacked, which ports need, are now relibc's own
(relibc-fixes.patch, see below).

Ports whose Genode glue is written for FreeBSD's libc get a build file of
their own here, which takes the place of the original in this build
directory only (the first repository's wins) and builds the original with
a difference: lighttpd (Genode's patch removes AT_FDCWD, relibc's stdio.h
would still select renameat2), libnl (dde_linux's libnl_emul.h defines
Linux socket types relibc has), wpa_supplicant and wpa_driver_nl80211
(glibc's byte-order macros; the emulated Linux constants relibc defines
as macros). They are src/app/lighttpd, lib/mk/libnl*.mk and
lib/mk/wpa_*.mk.

Known gaps, the 7 that do not compile: tests of FreeBSD's own API, which
a Linux-ABI libc does not have: test/libc (syscall(SYS_thr_self)),
libc_getaddrinfo (sockaddr's sa_len), libc_getpwent and libc_getgrent
(setpassent, setgroupent), libc_kqueue (kqueue), libc_vfs_tap
(net/if_tap.h), oss (FreeBSD's sys/soundcard.h).

relibc fixes made on the way (patches/relibc-fixes.patch, independent of
Genode, for upstream): %a/%A in
printf (and %La exactly for x86's 80-bit long double), %g's precision for
values below 1 and its exponent for values below f64::EPSILON, a second
_POSIX_THREADS in pthread.h, [[noreturn]] where GCC's C++ refuses it,
threads cancellable by default (as POSIX says), exit from another thread
than main (relibc cancelled the calling thread, too), FD_CLOEXEC as 1 on
Linux (relibc had O_CLOEXEC's value), sig_atomic_t as int (as
glibc), ioctl variadic (as glibc), if_indextoname writing to its buffer
and returning it (as POSIX), sem_wait failing with EAGAIN when the count
changed just before its futex wait (it retries now); and what it lacked:
getentropy (a stub before), arc4random, arc4random_buf and
arc4random_uniform, wcstof and wcstold, fileno_unlocked, finite and
finitef, accept4, and search.h (tsearch, tfind, tdelete, twalk, tdestroy).


In Sculpt
---------

relibc_hello runs in the sculpt-declarative image (SCULPT=declarative) as
the package sdecl/pkg/relibc_hello, exported with Genode's depot tool from
recipes/. Its output is in Sculpt's log (Bosun: Logs). After a change:

  tools/build-relibc                                   # if relibc changed
  cd ../genode
  REPOS="$(ls -d $PWD/repos/* | tr '\n' ' ') $(cd ../relibc-genode && pwd)"
  ./tool/depot/extract sdecl/pkg/relibc_hello "REPOSITORIES=$REPOS" UPDATE_VERSIONS=1 FORCE=1
  ./tool/depot/build sdecl/bin/x86_64/relibc_hello/<src version>    # recipes/src/relibc_hello/hash
  cd ../sculpt-declarative && sdecl pin relibc-hello && sdecl switch
  cd ../genode/build/x86_64 && make run/sculpt SCULPT=declarative

extract and build run separately: given to create, REPOSITORIES (needed
to find these recipes) would also reach the sandboxed bin build, which
then scans every repository. The pkg archive names genodelabs' vfs with
its version (recipes/pkg/relibc_hello/archives): the depot tool versions
only the exporting user's own archives, and an unversioned entry leaves
the package "incomplete" in Sculpt. used_apis are plain names, so the
user 'sdecl' gets build-time copies of genodelabs' API archives.


How it works
------------

relibc keeps its Linux ABI (types, constants, struct layouts, headers) and
is built for x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, with the cargo feature 'genode'
(the platform module relibc/src/platform/genode and
patches/relibc-genode.patch, on top of relibc-fixes.patch). The feature
replaces only relibc's system calls: its platform module
(src/platform/genode) calls the gxd_* functions of the shim instead.

Why a shim: on Linux, relibc's platform makes system calls, a stable ABI
of the kernel. Genode has none of the kind: files, timers, threads and the
LOG are C++ objects (Vfs::File_system, Timer::Connection, Genode::Thread)
reached through the component's Env, which Rust cannot call. The shim is
that layer in C++ and offers it as C functions, the gxd_* ones; gxd.rs
is their declaration, the counterpart of Linux's
system-call numbers. The platform module translates relibc's calls to
them and keeps what needs no Genode at all in Rust (signal actions and
masks, iovec gathering, file mappings as copies).

The platform module is laid out as relibc's Linux one: mod.rs (the Pal:
files, memory, time, threads, the process), epoll.rs, socket.rs,
signal.rs, ptrace.rs, and gxd.rs (the shim's C ABI). Each method is the
Linux one's call translated to the shim; what Genode has no counterpart
for returns ENOSYS. Outside of it, the 'genode' feature touches relibc in
few places, each a cfg next to the Linux code it replaces:

* Cargo.toml, platform/mod.rs: the feature and the platform's selection;
  chrono-tz's 'filter-by-regex' (tools/build-relibc builds in UTC and
  Etc/*, RELIBC_TIMEZONES for more: Genode copies a library's relocated
  data into every component, and the full table is 1 MiB of it)
* generic-rt, ld_so/mod.rs, ld_so/tcb.rs: the thread pointer is the
  shim's (gxd_get_tp, gxd_set_tp) instead of the fs register (see TLS)
* start.rs: no .init_array entry (see below)
* unistd/syscall.rs: syscall() returns ENOSYS
* pthread/mod.rs: pthread_create maps a page for the start arguments only
  and passes the stack size beside them (the thread runs on a Genode stack)
* pthread/attr.rs: the default stack size is Genode's libc's
  (Libc::Component::stack_size, 256 KiB), not 1 MiB: Genode backs a stack
  with RAM in full
* stdlib, cxa.rs, getopt: functions to forget the atexit functions and to
  reset getopt, for execve
* sync/cond.rs: pthread_cond_signal wakes one waiter (relibc wakes all
  "for now"; Genode's test-pthread checks that the others' timed waits
  time out)

Everything else is in the shim: pthread_cancel, for example, is relibc's
own (rlct_kill with its cancel signal), which the platform implements by
having the shim stop the thread; the nameserver for relibc's resolver is
an /etc/resolv.conf the shim provides where the VFS has none.

  C program ── relibc ──gxd_*──▶ shim (src/lib/relibc_genode) ──▶ LOG, Timer, Rtc,
                                                              VFS, RAM, CPU

The shim, one file per concern: state.h (shared state and locking),
start.cc (start and exit), posix_main.cc (main in a thread of its own),
ep.cc (the entrypoint's work for the other threads), fd.h/fd.cc (fds:
standard I/O, VFS files and directories as Linux dirent64 records, pipes,
the config's fds), epoll.cc, fs.cc (paths: resolution against the working
directory, unlink, mkdir, rename, ...), memory.cc (anonymous mappings),
time.cc, socket.cc (sockets through the VFS's socket file system),
signals.cc (pending signals, POSIX timers), thread.cc (threads, emulated
TLS, futexes, cancellation; exited threads are reaped by the next
pthread_create), exec.cc (execve), fork.cc (fork, posix_spawn, waitpid),
libc_component.cc (Genode's libc API). The shim has no static
constructors (a forked child would run them only at its execve).

Programs build with 'LIBS += relibc_genode' (lib/import/import-relibc_genode.mk):
relibc's headers, compiled with -D__relibc_linux_abi__ (they select the
Linux ABI by it), and relibc linked statically.

Starting: the shim's Component::construct builds the initial stack that
relibc expects from Linux (argc, argv and envp from the config's
'+ arg | value: ...' and '+ env | key: ... | value: ...', and an auxiliary
vector) and calls relibc_start_v1, which sets up TLS and relibc and
returns instead of running main. Then Libc::Component::construct runs, as
with Genode's libc: the posix library's (posix_main.cc) starts main in a
thread of its own, and exit ends the component through the parent, after
syncing the fds' pending writes (Genode's libc closes them).

The entrypoint: the component's VFS (files, terminals, file-system
sessions, an IP stack in the component) is used by the entrypoint alone,
which also dispatches its I/O signals, as with Genode's libc. Another
thread hands its VFS work over as a job and blocks (ep.cc); the entrypoint
executes it, again whenever the VFS reports progress
(Vfs::Env::User::wakeup_vfs_user), until it completes, and wakes the
thread. Nothing polls: deadlines (timed waits, poll timeouts, sleeping)
end through one timeout of the entrypoint at the earliest of them. Code
that runs on the entrypoint (a Libc::Component's) executes its jobs at
once and dispatches I/O signals while one is incomplete.

TLS: x86_64 programs normally reach thread-local variables through the fs
register, which NOVA and hw do not let a program set (no CR4.FSGSBASE, and
the fs base is not kept per thread). So relibc is built with emulated TLS
(-Z tls-model=emulated, as goa's Rust target for Genode does): every
thread-local is reached through __emutls_get_address. relibc's goes to
the shim's (libc.mk links it with --wrap=__emutls_get_address; the
dynamic linker's serves everything else), a table per thread in
anonymous memory, which a forked child inherits. relibc's thread pointer
(its TCB, generic-rt's 'genode' feature) is likewise a value the shim
keeps per thread (gxd_set_tp, gxd_get_tp). Nothing depends on the kernel,
and Genode itself is unchanged. The shim hands relibc a program header
table with an empty PT_TLS entry, which makes it set up its TCB.

Threads: relibc prepares a thread like for Linux's clone (entry point and
arguments on a stack it allocated, here only a page, with the size the
thread asked for beside them). The shim starts a Genode thread of that
stack size, which reads those words and makes the call. Futex waiters
queue per address and block on a Genode Blockade.

Processes, as with Genode's libc:

* execve: Genode's dynamic linker replaces the program within the
  component (Dynamic_linker::respawn); the libraries, the VFS and the fds
  (but those with FD_CLOEXEC) stay. relibc's platform forgets the
  program's atexit functions, signal handlers, getopt's position and other
  threads first. The program is the ROM module of its resolved path, which
  the VFS must show (e.g. '+ rom name'); "#!" names an interpreter.
* fork: the component becomes the parent of a child of the same binary.
  The parent serves it the binary's and libraries' ROM modules, its config
  (the parent's, with the fds, the working directory and the pid) and a
  clone session, through which the child copies the parent's memory: what
  relibc keeps (its data, apart from the shim's by relibc_state.ld), the
  data of the program and its libraries (those loaded before the VFS, but
  ld, libc, libm and vfs), the anonymous mappings (relibc's heap, TCBs and
  TLS), and the stack of the forking thread. The shim and its VFS the
  child builds from the config. The mappings lie in an address window of
  their own (16 TiB, 1 TiB), where the shim places them, so that the child
  puts them at the same addresses whatever else it attached before. The
  forking thread continues in the child on a thread with its stack at the
  same address: the thread of main (fork from another thread: ENOSYS).
* posix_spawn: a child of the program's ROM module, with the parent's
  config, the arguments, the environment, the working directory and the
  fds (relibc's file actions applied to the parent's in spawn.rs). Nothing
  is copied, and any thread may spawn.
* waitpid: the exit codes of both kinds of children; SIGCHLD when the
  program catches or blocks it (else it is ignored, as by default).
* Resources: a child gets 2.5 MB and 100 caps (as Genode's libc gives a
  forked child), a forked one also what it copies; more on its request,
  from the parent's quota.

relibc's .init_array constructor is not registered on Genode: Genode's
dynamic linker would run it before the component exists. relibc_start
calls it instead. Likewise the shim has no static objects with
constructors or destructors (their atexit registration would reach relibc
before it is initialized, and a forked child would run the constructors at
its execve).


Layout
------

  relibc/src/platform/genode/   relibc's Genode platform module (the Pal), as it
                                goes into relibc: for upstream as it is
  patches/relibc-fixes.patch    relibc bug fixes, independent of Genode
  patches/relibc-genode.patch   the 'genode' feature outside the platform module:
                                the few cfgs (both against patches/relibc.commit,
                                applied in this order)
  tools/get-relibc              clone relibc into ../relibc, apply the
                                patches, copy the platform module into it
  tools/save-relibc             the reverse, after editing ../relibc: the
                                platform module back, relibc-genode.patch anew
  tools/build-relibc            build relibc and its C headers into var/relibc/
  tools/nixos/shell.nix         the build environment on NixOS (SETUP)
  src/lib/relibc_genode/        the shim and the program start
  src/lib/relibc_genode/relibc_state.ld  relibc's data apart from the shim's (fork)
  lib/mk/relibc_genode.mk       the shim as a library
  lib/import/import-relibc_genode.mk  what programs on relibc get
  lib/mk/{libc,posix,libm}.mk   relibc as Genode's libc (see "relibc as the libc")
  lib/import/import-{libc,posix}.mk, lib/symbols/{libc,posix,libm}
  include/relibc-compat/        headers for code written against Genode's libc
  tools/mk-symbols              the ABI lists, from the built libraries
  src/app/lighttpd, lib/mk/{libnl,libnl_include,wpa_*}.mk, src/lib/{libnl,
  wpa_driver_nl80211}/include   Genode's ports on relibc (see above)
  src/test/relibc_hello/        a first test program
  src/test/relibc_posix/        the POSIX test (threads, files, pipes, poll)
  src/test/relibc_process/      the process test (posix_spawn, fork, execve,
                                waitpid, SIGCHLD), with relibc as the libc
  run/relibc_*.run              the tests
  run/pkg_test.run              Genode's test packages, on relibc (also those
                                that run an init of their own)
  run/relibc_lighttpd_fetchurl.run  lighttpd and curl, on relibc
  recipes/                      depot recipes: src and pkg of relibc_hello

Working on relibc: edit ../relibc (it is a git checkout at the base
commit, so 'git -C ../relibc diff' shows everything), build with
tools/build-relibc, and keep the changes with tools/save-relibc. A fix
that is independent of Genode belongs into patches/relibc-fixes.patch
(by hand; then tools/get-relibc on a fresh ../relibc and
tools/save-relibc). Moving to a newer relibc: change patches/relibc.commit,
remove ../relibc, run tools/get-relibc and resolve what does not
apply.

Toolchain: relibc's rust-toolchain.toml (nightly-2026-05-24 with rust-src),
through rustup, and cbindgen for the headers (var/tools/bin/cbindgen:
cargo install --root var/tools cbindgen, or CBINDGEN=...). tools/build-relibc calls that toolchain's binaries
directly, since the system's cargo and rustc come first on the PATH here.
